course: english
lesson: 3
title: Tricky words and common exception words
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most English words follow the sound rules, but some very common ones have an odd bit. Schools call them tricky words or common exception words, and the National Curriculum in England lists them for each year: «said», «was», «because», «people», «friend». They are odd mostly because they are old: the way people say them changed over hundreds of years, and the spelling stayed behind.
explain: A tricky word is almost never tricky all the way through. In «because» only the «au» is odd; in «said» only the «ai». So the smart move is to find the odd bit, underline it, and learn just that part, maybe with a trick: «Big Elephants Can Always Understand Small Elephants» spells BECAUSE.
explain: When Olivia writes «sed» for «said», she has listened perfectly; «sed» is exactly what we hear. That is a good phonics attempt, not a silly one. The fix is calm: «Great listening! This one is tricky: s-ai-d.»
explain: Tricky words come in families that share the odd bit: the, they, them, there, their all start with «the»; say, says, said share a strange vowel; come, some, done, none all spell the short /u/ sound with o-e. Learning a family together halves the work.
explain: Short, regular practice beats long battles. Five minutes a day with three to five words, as a game, works better than an hour of flashcards ending in tears. And one wrong word, like «frend» at seven, says nothing about dyslexia, a lasting difficulty with reading and spelling that only a specialist can identify.
fact: Common exception words are frequent words whose spelling does not fully match their sounds; the National Curriculum lists them for each year.
fact: Most of a tricky word still follows the rules; usually only one part is odd, like «au» in «because».
fact: Many tricky words are old words whose sound changed while the spelling stayed the same.
fact: Most English spelling is regular; only a small number of very common words are odd.
fact: A word wall in class shows key words so children can check them; using it is how spellings get learned, not cheating.
fact: Look, say, cover, write, check is a way to learn a spelling: look at it, say it, cover it, write it from memory, then check.
fact: Recognising a word when reading is easier than recalling its spelling, so children often read a word before they can spell it.
fact: Tricky words often come in families with the same odd part: say, says, said; come, some, done, none; the, they, there.
fact: Memory works better with short practice spread over days than with one long session.
fact: «Colour» is the British and Canadian school spelling, «color» the American one; neither is a mistake in its own country.
fact: «Friend» has «end» at the end: «a friend to the end».
fact: Dyslexia is a lasting difficulty with reading and spelling; only a specialist can identify it, never from one mistake.
fact: Slang in this lesson: «bostin'» means great and «tara-a-bit» means see you later in the Black Country near Birmingham; «sus» means suspicious or odd; «eh» is a word Canadians often put at the end of a sentence.
exercise: Jamie says «because», «people» and «beautiful» follow no rules at all. Which is closer to the truth? | Most of each word is regular; only one bit is odd | They are made-up words, so they have no rules | They were spelled wrong by mistake long ago and nobody fixed them | They follow the rules of French, not English
exercise: Olivia writes «sed» for «said». What does that show? | She listened well to the sounds | She has not learned any phonics | She is copying from a friend who can't spell | She is spelling it the American way
exercise: What does Leon in the Black Country mean by «bostin'»? | Great | Broken | Boring | Bossy, telling people what to do
exercise: Which group shares the same start? | the, they, there | was, saw, wash | said, sad, side | come, came, comb
exercise: Mo writes «wos». His teacher points at the word wall. Is looking at the wall cheating? | No, checking the wall is how spellings get learned | Yes, in class you must always spell from memory alone | Yes, but only during a spelling test | No, because the wall shows every word in English
exercise: Holly reads «you» as «yo-u» and her brother calls her «sus». What does «sus» mean? | Suspicious or odd | Super smart | Silly but sweet, said kindly | Sleepy
exercise: Dad says English spelling has «no rules at all» because of «one» and «two». What is true? | Most words follow patterns; a few old common ones are odd | He is right: no English word follows any rule | All English words are spelled exactly as they sound | Only number words are tricky; every other English word is spelled just as it sounds
exercise: Beth does flashcards for an hour a night and cries. What works better? | Five minutes a day with a few words | Two hours a night with more cards | One long session at the weekend only | Stopping all spelling practice for good
exercise: Aiden in Toronto sees «colour» and «color». Which one is a mistake? | Neither, each is right in its own country | «Colour», because it has an extra letter in it | «Color», because Canada uses British spelling only | Both, the real spelling is «culler»
exercise: Grace reads «come» and «some» but writes «cum» and «sum». Why? | Recalling a spelling is harder than recognising a word | She is careless and not really trying hard enough in class | Her teacher taught her the wrong spelling | «Cum» and «sum» are the newer spellings
exercise: Toby notices «says» sounds like «sez». Which word shares its odd vowel spelling? | said | sad | sees | seas
exercise: Bilal writes «frend». His nan asks if he is dyslexic. What is fair? | One mistake can't show that; it is very common at seven | Yes, one wrong word is enough to show it | No, dyslexia only affects maths, never spelling | Yes, because «friend» is the easiest word on the list
exercise: Which trick helps to spell «friend»? | A friend to the end | I before E after c | Big elephants are kind | Fry the end
exercise: What are the five steps of look, say, cover, write, check for? | Learning a spelling from memory | Reading a long word in chunks | Writing a letter to a friend | Checking your answers in maths at the end of a test
exercise: Why do many old common words have odd spellings? | Their sound changed but the spelling stayed | Printers chose random letters to save ink and paper | Children spelled them wrong and grown-ups copied them | They were borrowed from Chinese
exercise: In «because», which part is the odd bit? | au | be | se | c
test: «oi, look at the wall again and I'm tellin' Miss you're cheatin'». Is using the word wall cheating? | No, the wall is there so you check instead of guess | Yes, real spellers never look at anything | Yes, unless the teacher is out of the room and can't see you | No, but only Year 1 children may use it
test: Mum, cross: «SED?? rub it out, that's rubbish». What is a better response? | «Great listening! This one's tricky: s-ai-d» | «Rub it out and write it ten times» | «Leave it, sed is fine at your age, nobody will ever mind» | «Sed is the American way, so it's OK»
test: «bruh, one and two have literally no rules, english is mental». What is fair to say? | A few old words are odd, but most words follow patterns | He's right, every English word is a one-off | He's right, but only for number words | Wrong, «one» and «two» are spelled exactly how they sound
test: What would probably happen if Beth kept doing an hour of flashcards a night in tears? | She'd learn less, and spelling would feel like a battle | She would learn twice as fast as other children | She'd soon remember every tricky word in English | Nothing, how long you practise makes no difference
test: Aiden, eh: «my book's American, my teacher's Canadian, who's wrong?» | Nobody; color and colour are each right in their country | The book, American spelling is always wrong | The teacher, Canada uses only American spelling now | Both of them, the real spelling is «culler», like it sounds
test: «tara-a-bit, I'm off to learn they, then, them on me own as random shapes». What would help more? | Learning them as a family that all start with «the» | Learning each one as a random picture shape | Learning only one word a month, very slowly | Skipping them for now, they're far too short to matter in a story
test: Nan in Bradford: «frend! he's got that dyslexia, I'm sure of it». What should she do? | Teach «a friend to the end», and talk to the teacher only if problems are wide and lasting | Tell the school he has dyslexia | Stop him writing until the problem is fixed by itself | Buy him a computer so he never needs to spell again
test: A cartoon says tricky words must be learned «all at once, as a whole picture». What works better? | Find the odd bit and learn that part | Copy the whole word out a hundred times | Learn the word by its colour on the page | Only read tricky words, never write them
test: «lol yo-u?? ur so sus». How could Holly's brother help instead? | Show her you, your, you're, and how «you» stays the same in each | Keep laughing, so she remembers it next time | Tell her to skip the word when she meets it | Spell it for her every time she gets stuck
test: ★★★ Which word belongs in the family with come, some, done and none? | love | home | cone | move
test: ★★★ Grace can read 20 tricky words but spell only 12. How many can she read but not yet spell? | 8 | 12 | 20 | 32
test: ★★★ In which word do the letters «ai» make the same odd /e/ sound as in «said»? | again, as most British people say it | paid, as in «I paid for it» | rain, as in «it's raining» | sail, as in «a boat's sail»
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a common exception word? | A frequently used word of which some part departs from the usual correspondence of sound and letter | A word of which no letter whatsoever corresponds to its sound | A word employed exclusively in formal correspondence and in official documents of state | A word borrowed recently from a foreign tongue
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate response of a parent to a child who writes «sed» in place of «said». | To commend the accurate hearing of the sounds and demonstrate the correct spelling | To erase the attempt immediately and in evident displeasure | To accept the spelling permanently, since it faithfully reflects the sounds of the word as spoken | To refer the child to a specialist without delay
exam: Identify the principal reason for which numerous common English words possess irregular spellings. | Their pronunciation altered over centuries whilst their spelling remained unchanged | They were deliberately misspelled by scholars in order to test pupils | Their spelling is determined afresh each year by examination boards | They were invented recently and have not yet acquired settled spellings
exam: It is given that a pupil of seven years writes «frend» on one occasion. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | No conclusion concerning dyslexia may be drawn from a single common error | Dyslexia is thereby established beyond doubt | The pupil must be withdrawn from spelling instruction | The error demonstrates that the pupil has received no instruction
